cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite both the software and the article."
title: "ACP-SME: A Metadata-Driven Adaptive Cybersecurity Protector for SMEs — Reference Prototype and Reproduction Package"
abstract: >-
  ACP-SME (Adaptive Cybersecurity Protector for SMEs), version 0.1, is a
  privacy-oriented governance decision-support prototype that keeps a small or
  medium-sized enterprise's approved cybersecurity profile current as the
  business itself changes. It is the reference implementation and complete
  reproduction package for the accompanying article on resource-aware tailoring
  of NIST CSF 2.0, ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and CIS Controls v8.1.

  The artifact treats an approved profile as a versioned decision object. It
  implements the Minimum Necessary Metadata Model and its fail-closed local
  metadata guard, a typed and versioned capability-to-standards crosswalk, a
  material-change detector combining a weighted profile distance with
  deterministic critical predicates and a persistence rule, an exact
  resource-constrained profile selector, a hash-chained human-authorized profile
  ledger with role separation, and the seeded synthetic experiment over 90
  traces and 10,800 enterprise-days reported in the article.

  The core artifact, the experiment and the 140 tests run on a bare Python 3.9+
  standard library with no third-party dependencies; matplotlib is optional and
  needed only for figures.

  All results are synthetic model output. They verify internal behaviour under
  encoded assumptions and demonstrate neither real-world incident reduction, nor
  standards conformity, nor certification readiness. The software cannot
  implement a safeguard, change an approved profile or accept risk; only an
  authorized human decision does. No ISO/IEC control text is reproduced.

